<div style="width: 100%; padding: 20px; text-align: center; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 3 – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Managing Stress in Changing Times</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Breathing to Relax</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe a personal achievement. Interactive: Engage in storytelling sessions using personal experiences.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe personal achievements related to managing stress (e.g., "Practicing mindfulness has helped me stay calm during stressful times.")
            Can engage in storytelling by sharing personal experiences in managing stress and describing techniques like mindfulness (e.g., "Walking in the Metropolitan Natural Park is a peaceful way to manage stress for me.")
            Can answer questions about personal stress management practices, using examples to illustrate their effectiveness (e.g., "Listening to calming music helps me focus before exams.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts with a "Mindful Moment" activity. Students are asked to close their eyes (if comfortable) or gaze softly at a point, focusing on their breathing for one minute. After the minute, the teacher asks students to share how they feel using keywords like 'calm,' 'relaxed,' 'focused.' The teacher writes these words on the board, eliciting additional vocabulary such as 'serene,' 'peaceful,' 'stress-free' from prior lessons.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences for sharing feelings and giving simple advice: "I feel very *calm* after *breathing* deeply." "If you feel *overwhelmed*, *practicing deep breathing* is a helpful technique." "You *should try* to find a *quiet spot*." The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of the long vowel sound /uː/ in words like "soothing" and "smooth" by saying them clearly and having students repeat. They also highlight the stress and reduced vowels in "mindfulness" and "beneficial."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'practicing mindfulness has helped me,' what part of the sentence tells us *what* helped me?" (Expected answer: Practicing mindfulness) - Checks understanding of gerunds as subjects.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'You *should* try this technique,' am I telling you that you *must* do it, or that it's a *good idea*?" (Expected answer: A good idea/suggestion) - Checks understanding of modals for advice.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Managing Stress in Changing Times."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio dialogue (approximately 1.5-2 minutes) between two friends discussing how they cope with stress from school exams and social changes. The dialogue is informal, using a conversational register. It features examples of gerunds, modals for advice, and present continuous for trends, as well as the target vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner can understand the main ideas and some details of short, simple oral texts of a communicative and social nature, when the topic is familiar and predictable.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are the two friends mainly talking about?" (Expected: Stress management and relaxation techniques.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students listen again and mark T or F for statements based on the dialogue. E.g., "Friend A suggests *listening to music* to *calm* down." (T) "Friend B thinks *forest bathing* is a new trend in *wellness*." (T) "They are *practicing biohacks* to *improve* their *focus*." (F)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match stress-related problems mentioned by the friends with the proposed solutions, e.g., "Feeling *overwhelmed* before exams" matches "You *should try practicing deep breathing*."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                Grammar: Gerunds (as subjects, objects of prepositions), Modals for advice (should, could, might), Present continuous for trends.
                Vocabulary: breathing, relaxation, forest bathing, biohack, mindfulness, meditation, stress, wellness, nature, focus, music, exercise, retreats, techniques, practices, mind, body, balance, relief, habits, quiet spot, practice, relax, meditate, calm, reduce, improve, benefit, promote, connect, focus, exercise, listen, try, suggest, encourage, relieve, manage, gain, participate, relaxing, mindful, deep, soothing, popular, beneficial, calming, traditional, outdoors, natural, helpful, wellness-focused, effective, stress-free, peaceful, serene, stressful, sensory, overwhelmed.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Gerunds & Modals Cloze):</b> Students work individually to complete sentences with appropriate gerunds or modals based on context. Example: "___ (Practice) *mindfulness* is a common way to find inner *peace*." (Practicing) "If you feel *overwhelmed*, you ___ (try) *listening* to *calming music*." (should try) "___ (Walk) in *nature* is *beneficial* for your *mind* and *body*." (Walking). Teacher monitors for accurate use of gerunds and modals and provides immediate feedback on pronunciation, especially /uː/ sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-controlled (Suggestion Swap):</b> In pairs, students take turns making suggestions for different stress-related scenarios using modals for advice and gerunds. The teacher provides cue cards with scenarios (e.g., "Feeling sleepy in class," "Can't focus on homework," "Having trouble sleeping"). Student A says, "I'm feeling really *stressed* about my grades." Student B responds, "You *could try practicing deep breathing* before *studying*," or "You *should think about getting* enough sleep. *Relaxing* is key." They are encouraged to use phrases like "In my experience..." and a respectful tone.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction (Sharing Wellness Habits):</b> Students interview their partner using a short list of questions: "What personal *wellness* *habits* do you *practice* to *manage stress*?" "What *techniques* do you *suggest* for someone feeling *overwhelmed*?" "Are *people* *practicing forest bathing* more often in your community?" They must include at least two gerunds and one modal for advice in their answers, focusing on expressing themselves clearly and using the target vocabulary. The teacher circulates, noting down common errors in grammar or pronunciation for later whole-class correction.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">20 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Managing Stress in Changing Times."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Stress-Free Story" (Storytelling Session).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> In small groups of 3-4, students will engage in a storytelling session. Each student will share a personal story (2-3 minutes) about an achievement related to *managing stress* or a personal experience where they successfully used a *relaxation technique*. They must describe the situation, the technique used (e.g., *practicing mindfulness*, *listening to calming music*, *forest bathing*), and the *beneficial* outcome. They are encouraged to use gerunds (e.g., "Before *starting* school, *meditating* helped me."), modals for advice (e.g., "You *should try* *connecting* with *nature*."), and the present continuous for trends (e.g., "Many *people* *are promoting* *wellness* *retreats*."). Group members listen actively, ask follow-up questions politely (e.g., "Could you tell us more about *deep breathing*?"), and offer positive feedback, focusing on collaborative interaction and using personal experiences to illustrate points. The goal is to share and learn from each other's *stress management practices* in a supportive environment, describing techniques using examples to illustrate their effectiveness.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "My Stress-Free Story" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the "My Stress-Free Story" storytelling session, describing personal achievements and experiences related to stress management.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially of /uː/ and reduced vowels in multisyllabic words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (at least 5-7 words from the list).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of gerunds and modals for advice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence in describing experiences and giving advice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active and respectful participation in collaborative interaction.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a small slip of paper before leaving:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I successfully shared my experience about ____________________, using phrases like ____________________."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new idea for *managing stress* I learned from my classmates is ____________________. I *might try practicing* it."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel now about *describing* my *personal achievements* in *managing stress* and *giving advice* on *relaxation techniques*?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of *sharing* my story or *listening* to others helped me the most in *improving* my *speaking skills* today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The speaking activities fostered good interaction and allowed students to genuinely share personal experiences. Students were able to use gerunds and modals effectively, especially when *offering suggestions politely*. Some students still need more practice with the pronunciation of reduced vowels in longer words like "beneficial." For the next lesson, which focuses on writing, I need to provide more explicit sentence frames to guide their composition, building on the oral output from today's storytelling. I will also incorporate prompts that encourage *describing* detailed *relaxation techniques* and their *benefits* in written form.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This speaking lesson provided students with opportunities to articulate their experiences and advice regarding stress management. In Lesson 4 (Writing), students will build upon these shared ideas and vocabulary to write a short paragraph describing their preferred stress management techniques and a personal achievement related to their *wellness* journey, using the grammatical structures practiced today. This will reinforce their understanding and allow them to express themselves formally in writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">2 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>